WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
 Usually, when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of clinical precision.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:06.800
 Right.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:07.800
 That gets very cut and dry.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.800
 Yeah.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:09.800
 Exactly.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.800
 It feels almost like engineering.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.240
 You fall off your bike, you break your arm, the x-ray shows that jagged white line and

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.880
 the doctor just points at the screen and says, "Well, there it is.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:17.880
 That's the problem."

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.280
 It's a very binary way of looking at medicine.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.720
 Like it's broken or not broken, normal or abnormal.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.720
 Yeah.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.300
 You only like things to be visible, neatly categorized and easily fixed.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.500
 But then, I mean, you step into the world of neurology, of neurodevelopment and brain

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.920
 trauma, and suddenly that trusty x-ray machine feels just completely inadequate.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.200
 Oh, totally inadequate.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.200
 Right.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.200
 We're looking at a diagnostic landscape that is incredibly murky.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.760
 It's not just about finding a broken bone.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.120
 It's about understanding an entire enclosed ecosystem that is just constantly shifting.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.840
 The central nervous system is the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.240
 I mean, a patient can have a structurally perfect brain on a scan.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.240
 Right.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.800
 But functionally, they might be in a life-threatening crisis because of, say, a microscopic chemical

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.440
 imbalance or a slight shift in pressure that we can't even see with a naked eye.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.440
 Which is wild.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.440
 So, welcome to the deep dive.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.080
 Today, our mission is to explore those muddy waters.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.080
 Yeah.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.080
 It's a huge topic.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.080
 It is.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.880
 Hence, highly detailed nursing study guide on neurology, covering chapters 60 to 65.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.520
 We're going to decode the most complex machine in the universe, the human brain.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.720
 But we're doing it specifically through the lens of critical care nursing.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.400
 Which I think offers a totally different vantage point than, say, a purely surgical text.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.400
 Right.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.880
 Because nurses aren't just looking at the overarching medical diagnosis, are they?

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:38.880
 Exactly.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.800
 They are tasked with managing the minute-to-minute environment of the patient.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.720
 They're the ones on the front lines trying to keep this delicate enclosed system from

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.320
 cascading into failure.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.960
 And for you listening right now, whether you're a medical professional studying for your boards

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.760
 or just someone who, you know, gets light-headed seeing a band-aid, this matters to you.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.760
 It really does.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.200
 Understanding how your brain balances itself, how medical teams can physically peer inside

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.800
 your skull, and what happens when those automated systems fail, it gives you a profound appreciation

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.080
 for your own biology.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.080
 Yeah.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.000
 I mean, every thought you're having right now, as you process these words, is a result

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.200
 of the invisible magic we're about to discuss.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.200
 So true.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.800
 And really, the overarching theme of this material is management.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.160
 The human brain is incredibly powerful, arguably the most powerful processor on earth.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.720
 But it's also incredibly fragile.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:29.720
 Right.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.640
 It's confined with an rigid bone box, and it relies entirely on a constant, uninterrupted

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.880
 supply of oxygen and glucose.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.520
 So before we get into the disasters, like the strokes, the traumas, the infections, let's

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.620
 look at how the brain operates on just, you know, a normal Tuesday.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.620
 The baseline.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.620
 Right.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.620
 The baseline.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.160
 The study guide starts heavily with this concept of homeostasis.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.160
 The brain is constantly working behind the scenes to keep everything perfectly balanced.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.160
 Yeah.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.920
 So homeostasis is essentially the body's relentless, automated drive to maintain a steady internal

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:01.920
 state.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
 Like a thermostat.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:03.920
 Exactly.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.160
 Like an internal thermostat.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.440
 But it's regulating far more than just your body temperature.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.760
 It's constantly adjusting your blood pH, so your blood doesn't become too acidic.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.960
 It's tweaking your cardiac output, monitoring your electrolyte levels, your blood glucose,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.320
 and crucially for the brain, your intracranial pressure.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.480
 And managing all of these dials and levers falls largely to the autonomic nervous system.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.640
 The guide splits this into two opposing teams, basically, the sympathetic and the parasympathetic.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.000
 Yes, the classic tug of war.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.600
 Let's trace how these actually work in the body.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.240
 So the sympathetic nervous system is our fight or flight response, right?

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.240
 Right.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.240
 The adrenergic system.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.960
 It's driven primarily by the neurotransmitters epinephrine and norepinephrine.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.760
 Adrenaline, basically.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.760
 Exactly.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.280
 Evolutionarily, this is what kicks in when you're startled by a predator.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.320
 If you're walking in the woods and see a bear, your sympathetic nervous system instantly

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.320
 takes over.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.920
 It just floods the system.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:00.920
 Yeah.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.440
 It dilates your pupils so you can let in more light to see the threat.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.360
 It increases your heart rate and the force of your heart's contractions to put blood

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.600
 to your major muscles.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.760
 Getting you ready to run.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.760
 Right.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.200
 And it dilates the bronchules in your lungs so you can pull in more oxygen to run away.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.120
 And it shuts down anything that isn't immediately useful for survival, like you aren't digesting

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.480
 your lunch while you're running from a bear.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.480
 Exactly.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.480
 Yeah.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.560
 Which is the exact opposite of the parasympathetic nervous system, which we call the rest and

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.840
 digest or colonergic system.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.360
 Driven by acetylcholine, right?

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.440
 You got it.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.980
 When the threat is gone and you're sitting safely on your couch, the parasympathetic

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.440
 system takes over.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.440
 It slows the heart rate, constricts the pupils to normal size, and directs blood flow back

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.880
 to your gastrointestinal tract so you can process nutrients.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.400
 So these two systems are in a constant, delicate tug of war to maintain that homeostasis.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.400
 Absolutely.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.440
 That's the baseline operating system, but looking at the physical structure of the brain itself,

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.160
 the study guide advises not to get bogged down, memorizing every single tiny crevice,

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.480
 but to understand the broad strokes.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.480
 Right.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.480
 The macro view.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.480
 Yeah.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.120
 Like the frontal lobe is handling our executive function, our decision making, our impulses.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.360
 The occipital lobe in the back is processing our vision.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.240
 And the temporal lobes are doing hearing and language, parietal, sensory, right?

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.240
 All the different compartments.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.800
 But the detail that really made me pause was the hierarchy of anatomy.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.080
 The physical structure of the brain actually dictates how the brain dies.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.480
 It's a really sobering physiological reality.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.600
 To understand it, you have to picture the brain's development.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.600
 Okay.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.400
 The deepest, lowest part of the brain, the brain stem, is the most primitive.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.000
 It evolved first.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.120
 It controls the absolute basics of biological survival.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.840
 So your drive to breathe, your heart rate, your blood pressure.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.840
 Exactly.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.800
 And then layered on top of that primitive core, we have the cerebral cortex, the frontal,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.720
 the temporal lobes, the newer stuff, the higher level of functions, yeah, your personality,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.880
 your memories, your ability to play the piano or speak a language, all of that resides in

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.440
 the outer upper layers of the cerebral cortex.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.440
 Right.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.200
 So when the brain experiences a severe insult, like a lack of oxygen or degenerative disease,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.560
 it typically fails from the outside in man.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.320
 So it's like a house and a severe hurricane.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.800
 Oh, that's a good way to look at it.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:22.800
 Yeah.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:23.800
 Think about it.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.800
 And the upper floor is where all your personal belongings are.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.080
 They take the first hit from the wind, they get ripped away first.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.480
 But the brain stem, that primitive core, is like a reinforced concrete basement with

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.640
 an industrial backup generator.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:41.600
 It just keeps the essential power on and keeps the heart beating, even if the entire rest

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.680
 of the house has been destroyed.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.240
 That analogy perfectly illustrates the tragic trajectory of conditions like Alzheimer's

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.640
 or severe dementia.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:50.640
 It really does.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.320
 Families often struggle to understand why their loved one has lost all memory, decision-making

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.120
 ability and personality.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.600
 The entire upper floors of the house are gone.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.040
 Yet they continue to breathe normally, digest food and have a regular heartbeat.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.320
 Because that basement generator is still going.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:07.320
 Yes.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.800
 The basement generator of the brain stem is still running perfectly, even though the cognitive

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.320
 essence of the person has been stripped away.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.960
 It reframes dimension in such a physical way.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:20.520
 So if we want to look inside this house to assess the damage or find a problem, we have

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.040
 to rely on diagnostics.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.040
 Right.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.960
 And the nursing guide details, an entire arsenal of neuroimaging, let's start with a CT scan.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.840
 The main advantage here seems to be speed.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.080
 Speed is everything in a neurological emergency.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:39.760
 A CT scan, or computed tomography, uses x-rays to create cross-sectional images of the brain.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:40.760
 And it's fast.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:45.160
 It takes just a few minutes, making it the absolute gold standard for a patient who rolls

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.920
 into the ER with head trauma or a suspected stroke.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:53.640
 But the guide includes a major caveat, CT scans take thick slices.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.800
 Let me puzzle through what that actually means clinically.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.800
 Go for it.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.600
 If the scanner is taking an image every few millimeters, it's not a continuous seamless

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.600
 picture.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:03.800
 No, it's not.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.200
 Think of slicing a loaf of bread.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.200
 Okay.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:10.880
 If you take very thick slices, say an inch thick, you might have a small pocket of mold

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.840
 completely hidden within the middle of one of those thick slices.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.000
 Oh, wow.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.400
 You wouldn't see it on the surface.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.400
 Exactly.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.600
 The CT scan operates with a similar limitation.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:27.680
 Because the visual slices have a certain thickness, a very tiny blood clot or a minuscule microbleed

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.480
 or a very early stage tumor can easily be missed.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:36.520
 So a negative CT scan in the ER is great news, but it doesn't definitively rule out every

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:38.000
 microscopic problem.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.000
 Right.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.920
 It's a screening tool for the big stuff.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.480
 And if the doctors need a better look at the blood vessels themselves, they use contrast

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.160
 dye during the CT.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:51.400
 But from a nursing perspective, giving contrast creates a whole new set of responsibilities

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.400
 afterward, right?

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.480
 You can't just send them back to their room and forget about it.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:56.480
 Definitely not.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.320
 The contrast dye is nephrotoxic, meaning it can be very damaging to the kidneys if it's

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.120
 allowed to sit and concentrate in the renal system.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.440
 So what's the move?

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:10.200
 The nurse's immediate priority post scan is flushing that dye out of the patient's body.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:16.040
 If the patient is going home, the instruction is to drink a massive bottle of water immediately.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.040
 And flush the pipes.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.040
 Yep.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:22.520
 If they're admitted, the nurse will run IV fluids to force the kidneys to process and

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.240
 excrete the contrast.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.160
 We see that same theme of contrast dye with cerebral angiography.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.160
 This one sounds incredibly invasive.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.160
 It is.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.000
 They're looking for aneurysms or narrowed blood vessels in the brain, but the entry

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.080
 point is usually the femoral artery down in the groin.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.640
 Yeah, they puncture the femoral artery, which is a massive high pressure vessel.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.280
 And it thred a catheter all the way up through the aorta, into the neck, and up into the

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.240
 cerebral circulation.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.440
 That's a long journey for a wire.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.120
 It is, but it provides a flawless road map of the brain's plumbing, but you are absolutely

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.920
 right about the invasiveness.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.320
 So when the procedure's over and they pull that catheter out of the groin, the nurse isn't

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.280
 just slapping a band-aid on it.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:07.960
 No, a band-aid would be blown off in seconds.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.480
 The arterial pressure in the femoral artery is immense.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.120
 It's like a fire hose.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.360
 Precisely.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.560
 The nurse must hold strict manual pressure on that puncture site, sometimes for 15 to

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.320
 20 minutes, just to allow a primary clot to form.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:23.960
 Just leaning on it.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:25.320
 Leaning hard.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:29.040
 And after that, the patient is placed on strict flat bed rest for hours.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.600
 The leg must be kept perfectly straight.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.680
 Because if they bend it, if they bend their hip, they can crack that fragile clot open,

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.600
 and they can literally bleed to death internally into their leg or retroperitoneal space before

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.600
 anyone notices.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:41.600
 Man.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.480
 Compare that high-stakes physical intervention to an MRI.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.960
 Magnetic resonance imaging gives a much clearer, more detailed picture of the brain tissue

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:53.120
 itself without any radiation, which is great.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.720
 It's beautiful imaging.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.840
 But the patient experience is notoriously difficult.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.960
 You're sliding into a tight, incredibly loud tube for anywhere from 30 to 60 minutes.

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:06.720
 The nursing guide specifically flags claustrophobia as a major barrier here.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.200
 It's often the nurse's responsibility to anticipate this.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.320
 Because if they panic, the scan is ruined.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:13.320
 Right.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.360
 If a patient is anxious, the nurse needs to advocate for them and ask the provider for

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.600
 an anti-anxiety medication like a benzodiazepine beforehand.

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.840
 Because if the patient moves even a fraction of an inch during that hour, the images are

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.880
 blurred and totally useless.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.640
 And the other non-negotiable rule?

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.520
 Absolutely no metal, none.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:35.140
 The magnetic field generated by an MRI machine is tens of thousands of times stronger than

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:36.920
 the magnetic field of the Earth.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:37.920
 It's insane.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.960
 It will violently pull any ferromagnetic metal.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:46.040
 We're talking about pacemakers, or an aneurysm clips, and even some permanent jewelry.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:50.000
 If a patient has an implanted device that isn't MRI safe, putting them in that scanner

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.000
 can be fatal.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.240
 Then you have the PET scan, which is fascinating because it doesn't just show the physical

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.360
 anatomy, the structure of the house.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.080
 It shows the activity inside the house.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:03.800
 Positron emission tomography measures metabolic activity.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.480
 They inject a radioactive tracer, usually attached to a glucose analog.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:09.480
 Sugar basically.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:10.480
 Right.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.680
 Because the brain relies entirely on glucose for energy, areas of the brain that are highly

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.400
 active will gobble up that radioactive glucose.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.480
 They just light up on the scan.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:20.480
 Yes.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:24.240
 This allows us to see not just what the brain looks like, but which parts are working, which

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.040
 parts are damaged, or where a highly metabolic tumor is aggressively growing.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:34.000
 Okay, those are the high-tech imaging machines, but the diagnostic procedure that really caught

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.740
 my eye purely because of the mechanical physics involved is the lumbar puncture, the LP, often

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:41.040
 called a spinal tap.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.080
 It's a classic procedure.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.600
 They're going in to get cerebrospinal fluid, or CSF, usually to check for infections like

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.000
 meningitis.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:54.160
 But the physical positioning required for this, you're putting a needle into the spine.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:56.880
 The anatomy of the spine requires absolute precision.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.560
 The spinal cord itself runs down through the vertebrae, but it typically ends around the

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.000
 first or second lumbar vertebra.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.040
