00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:51.360
 And all of this, the changing LOC, the sluggish pupils, it all comes back to one inescapable

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:54.840
 physical reality of human anatomy, the skull.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:56.200
 The bone box.

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:59.480
 The skull is a rigid, unforgiving bone box.

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:00.480
 It does not stretch.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.560
 It does not expand to accommodate extra volume.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:07.920
 And this brings us to what feels like the most important physics lesson in all of neurology,

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.880
 the Monroe-Kelly hypothesis.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:13.080
 It is the foundational principle of neurocritical care.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:18.120
 The Monroe-Kelly hypothesis dictates that the rigid cranial vault contains exactly three

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:19.120
 components.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:20.120
 What are they?

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:25.960
 Roughly 80% brain tissue, 10% blood, and 10% cerebrospinal fluid.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:31.020
 As the skull cannot expand, except in infants whose fontanels haven't fused, the total

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:34.680
 volume inside that box must remain absolutely constant.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:38.160
 Let's build an analogy for this, because the math is so crucial.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:42.960
 So think of the skull like a hard shell suitcase you're packing for a trip.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.560
 Right, a very rigid suitcase.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:45.560
 Exactly.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:47.840
 And you've packed it to the absolute brim.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:53.160
 It's perfectly full, like 80% close, which is the brain tissue, and then 10% shoes, so

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:57.480
 that's the blood, and 10% toiletries, the CSF.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.400
 The cerebrospinal fluid, right?

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:00.400
 Right.

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:02.920
 And you just managed to force that zipper closed.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:05.040
 A perfect equilibrium.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:09.600
 But then you decide you need to pack one more thick, heavy sweater.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:14.120
 Let's call that new sweater a growing brain tumor, or a sudden pool of blood from a ruptured

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:19.040
 vessel, or just generalized swelling of the brain tissue after a trauma.

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:20.400
 The new volume.

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:25.240
 If you shove that thick sweater into the hard shell suitcase, you cannot close the zipper.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:29.000
 The only physical way to get the suitcase closed is to remove something else.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.720
 You have to take out a pair of shoes, or you have to take out your shampoo.

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:34.800
 That is exactly what the brain tries to do initially.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:36.280
 It's called compensation.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:37.280
 Okay.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:42.080
 If a mass starts growing, the body will try to shunt some of the cerebrospinal fluid down

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:45.440
 out of the skull and into the spinal canal.

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:46.640
 It removes the toiletries.

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:47.640
 Right.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.760
 It might slightly constrictly cerebrovains to reduce the volume of venous blood inside

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:53.760
 the head.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:54.760
 It removes the shoes.

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:59.440
 But the suitcase only has so many shoes and toiletries eventually run out of things to

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:00.440
