00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.800
 And that is when compensation fails.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.800
 When the brain can no longer displace fluid or blood to make room for the swelling, the

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:10.240
 pressure inside the rigid box begins to rise exponentially.

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:13.080
 That's your intracranial pressure, or ICP.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.120
 And normal is what?

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:19.840
 normal ICP is heavily regulated between 10 and 20 millimeters of mercury.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:24.120
 And we care so deeply about our rising ICP because of how it ruins the next mathematical

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:28.520
 equation, cerebral perfusion pressure, or CPP.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:31.640
 This is the equation that dictates life or death for brain cells.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:34.160
 Let me see if I have the physiological logic, right?

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:35.160
 Sure.

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:39.120
 For the brain to survive, fresh, oxygenated arterial blood has to be pumped into the skull

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:40.120
 by the heart.

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:41.120
 Correct.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:44.060
 And the pressure of that blood being pumped into the system is essentially your mean

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:46.360
 arterial pressure, your map.

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:50.800
 But if the pressure inside the skull, the ICP is getting higher and higher, the heart

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:55.000
 has to push harder and harder against that internal resistance to get the blood in.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.560
 It's fighting the pressure.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:02.080
 So CPP is the actual net pressure of the blood successfully reaching the brain tissue.

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:05.800
 The equation is map minus ICP equals CPP.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.040
 Yes.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:12.160
 The study guide stresses that the magic number for a healthy CPP is generally around 60 to

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:13.560
 70 millimeters of mercury.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:14.560
 Okay.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:19.240
 If your CPP drops below 60, the brain tissue isn't getting enough blood flow.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:21.080
 Starting to starve for oxygen.

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:26.800
 If the CPP drops below 50, you are looking at severe, permanent, irreversible, ischemic

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:28.040
 brain damage.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:29.920
 So let's run the math on a patient.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:33.400
 Let's say a patient has a normal, healthy map of 65.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:38.160
 For their kidneys, their liver, their heart, a map of 65 is totally fine.

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:41.640
 But they have a brain injury and their suitcase is overflowing.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.880
 Their ICP has spiked to 20.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:49.680
 So map of 65 minus an ICP of 20 equals a CPP of 45.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:50.680
 Wow.

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:54.160
 So even though the monitor shows a totally acceptable normal blood pressure for the rest

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:57.600
 of their body, their brain is secretly suffocating.

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:58.600
 Exactly.

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:02.040
 The resistance inside the skull is too high for the blood to effectively perfuse the

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:03.440
 tissue.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:08.000
 And this is why critical care nurses are absolutely terrified of rising ICP.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.560
 Because it's a silent killer.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:13.760
 The early signs, as we discussed, are subtle changes in LSC, maybe a worsening headache

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:15.840
 or unexpected vomiting.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:21.040
 But if you miss those early signs, the pressure continues to build until you hit the late signs.

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.000
